#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701137 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s$, where $f(x,y,z)=4 x - 2 y^{2} + 2 z^{2}$ and\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t^{2}, t - 1, t + 2\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nDo not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate each coordinate and compute the speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle 2 t, 1, 1\\rangle \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(2 t)^2+(1)^2+(1)^2}=\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\nSubstitute the coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=4 t^{2} - 2 \\left(t - 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(t + 2\\right)^{2} \\\\ &=4 t^{2} + 12 t + 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply by speed and retain the parameter bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{2}f(\\mathbf r(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2}(4 t^{2} + 12 t + 6)(\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 2})\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2}2 \\sqrt{2} \\sqrt{2 t^{2} + 1} \\left(2 t^{2} + 6 t + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701139 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s$, where $f(x,y,z)=- x + y - z$ and\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t^{2} + 2, t^{2} - 2, t^{2}\\rangle,\\qquad 1\\le t\\le 3.\n$$\n\nDo not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate each coordinate and compute the speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle 2 t, 2 t, 2 t\\rangle \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(2 t)^2+(2 t)^2+(2 t)^2}=2 \\sqrt{3} \\left|{t}\\right|=2 \\sqrt{3} t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge0$ on this interval, $|t|=t$.\n\nSubstitute the coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=- t^{2} - 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply by speed and retain the parameter bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{3}f(\\mathbf r(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{3}(- t^{2} - 4)(2 \\sqrt{3} t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{3}- 2 \\sqrt{3} t \\left(t^{2} + 4\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701165 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s$, where $f(x,y,z)=4 x - 2 y^{2} + 2 z^{2}$ and\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t^{2}, t - 1, t - 1\\rangle,\\qquad 1\\le t\\le 3.\n$$\n\nDo not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate each coordinate and compute the speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle 2 t, 1, 1\\rangle \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(2 t)^2+(1)^2+(1)^2}=\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\nSubstitute the coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=4 t^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply by speed and retain the parameter bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{3}f(\\mathbf r(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{3}(4 t^{2})(\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 2})\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{3}4 \\sqrt{2} t^{2} \\sqrt{2 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701167 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s$, where $f(x,y)=- \\sqrt{2} \\left(x + y\\right)$ and\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t^{2} + t, t^{2} - t\\rangle,\\qquad 1\\le t\\le 3.\n$$\n\nDo not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate each coordinate and compute the speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle 2 t + 1, 2 t - 1\\rangle \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(2 t + 1)^2+(2 t - 1)^2}=\\sqrt{8 t^{2} + 2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\nSubstitute the coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=- 2 \\sqrt{2} t^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply by speed and retain the parameter bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{3}f(\\mathbf r(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{3}(- 2 \\sqrt{2} t^{2})(\\sqrt{8 t^{2} + 2})\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{3}- 4 t^{2} \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701179 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\left(- x^{2} + 3 y + 1\\right)\\,dx+\\left(- x - y^{2} + 1\\right)\\,dy\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the boundary of the rectangle $-1\\le x\\le 0$, $-1\\le y\\le 1$, traversed counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve. The components have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, so\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=- x^{2} + 3 y + 1$ and $Q=- x - y^{2} + 1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=3,\\\\\nQ_x&=-1,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=-4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe supplied rectangular bounds give independent limits.\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 0,\\ -1\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nThe shaded region and counterclockwise boundary are shown below.\n\n![](images/e1/e1ae198b1e8fd19a33c42fa66a5ce69cc9be0529767d8cca87b0e9df12cc2ce1.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy&=\\int_{-1}^{0}\\int_{-1}^{1}\\left(-4\\right)\\,dy\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_{-1}^{0}\\left[- 4 y\\right]_{-1}^{1}\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_{-1}^{0}\\left(-8\\right)\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\left[- 8 x\\right]_{-1}^{0}\\\\\n&=0-\\left(8\\right)\\\\\n&=-8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701188 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\left(- x^{2} + 3 y + 1\\right)\\,dx+\\left(3 x - y^{2} + 1\\right)\\,dy\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the boundary of the rectangle $0\\le x\\le 3$, $0\\le y\\le 2$, traversed counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve. The components have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, so\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=- x^{2} + 3 y + 1$ and $Q=3 x - y^{2} + 1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=3,\\\\\nQ_x&=3,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe supplied rectangular bounds give independent limits.\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 3,\\ 0\\le y\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nThe shaded region and counterclockwise boundary are shown below.\n\n![](images/e0/e07eeb2dd11a7b0a8ca4ba69ecff2adb6928c6d8a41883c4b1113271f437b85a.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy&=\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(0\\right)\\,dy\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{3}\\left[0\\right]_{0}^{2}\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(0\\right)\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\left[0\\right]_{0}^{3}\\\\\n&=0-\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701189 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\left(2 x^{2} + 3 y + 1\\right)\\,dx+\\left(x + 2 y^{2} + 1\\right)\\,dy\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the boundary of the rectangle $-2\\le x\\le 1$, $-1\\le y\\le 2$, traversed counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve. The components have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, so\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=2 x^{2} + 3 y + 1$ and $Q=x + 2 y^{2} + 1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=3,\\\\\nQ_x&=1,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=-2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe supplied rectangular bounds give independent limits.\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-2\\le x\\le 1,\\ -1\\le y\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nThe shaded region and counterclockwise boundary are shown below.\n\n![](images/4f/4f1cbdcf9782074201b30807132c3750c91dc4666e6ef81f1da8f10494caa0e7.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy&=\\int_{-2}^{1}\\int_{-1}^{2}\\left(-2\\right)\\,dy\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_{-2}^{1}\\left[- 2 y\\right]_{-1}^{2}\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_{-2}^{1}\\left(-6\\right)\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\left[- 6 x\\right]_{-2}^{1}\\\\\n&=-6-\\left(12\\right)\\\\\n&=-18.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701193 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\left(x^{2} - 3 y + 1\\right)\\,dx+\\left(3 x + y^{2} + 1\\right)\\,dy\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the boundary of the rectangle $-1\\le x\\le 0$, $1\\le y\\le 3$, traversed counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve. The components have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, so\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=x^{2} - 3 y + 1$ and $Q=3 x + y^{2} + 1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=-3,\\\\\nQ_x&=3,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe supplied rectangular bounds give independent limits.\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 0,\\ 1\\le y\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nThe shaded region and counterclockwise boundary are shown below.\n\n![](images/ae/ae6b8a19722dcba295986f9b20858ccdb1d96b47a1cd71cf95b8284cdd8ca633.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy&=\\int_{-1}^{0}\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(6\\right)\\,dy\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_{-1}^{0}\\left[6 y\\right]_{1}^{3}\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_{-1}^{0}\\left(12\\right)\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\left[12 x\\right]_{-1}^{0}\\\\\n&=0-\\left(-12\\right)\\\\\n&=12.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701198 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\left(x^{2} - y + 1\\right)\\,dx+\\left(2 x + y^{2} + 1\\right)\\,dy\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the boundary of the rectangle $0\\le x\\le 2$, $1\\le y\\le 3$, traversed counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve. The components have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, so\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=x^{2} - y + 1$ and $Q=2 x + y^{2} + 1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=-1,\\\\\nQ_x&=2,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe supplied rectangular bounds give independent limits.\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\ 1\\le y\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nThe shaded region and counterclockwise boundary are shown below.\n\n![](images/bc/bc90e6b5add5bcbe2131a4a86cd625e216b6663e4a79b24ae38f8e7868d488f2.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(3\\right)\\,dy\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left[3 y\\right]_{1}^{3}\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(6\\right)\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\left[6 x\\right]_{0}^{2}\\\\\n&=12-\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=12.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701206 :question/problem "The curves $y=x^2$ and $y=x+2$ enclose a region $D$. Its boundary $C$ is traversed counterclockwise. For\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle \\ln(x+2)-y^2,\\,x^2+xy+y^2\\rangle,\n$$\n\nuse Green's theorem to express $\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r$ as a repeated integral, integrating with respect to $y$ first. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For a counterclockwise, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve $C$ bounding $D$, Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe component functions here have continuous first partial derivatives on an open set containing $D$. Writing $\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r=P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y$, we have $P=\\ln(x+2)-y^2$ and $Q=x^2+xy+y^2$. Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=-2y,\\\\Q_x&=2x+y,\\\\Q_x-P_y&=(2x+y)-(-2y)\\\\&=2x+3y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe logarithm contributes nothing to $P_y$ because it depends only on $x$.\n\nTo find the outer limits, solve for the intersections:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^2&=x+2,\\\\(x-2)(x+1)&=0,\\\\x&=-1\\ \\text{or}\\ x=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBetween these values,\n\n$$\n(x+2)-x^2=(2-x)(x+1)\\ge0,\n$$\n\nso the parabola is the lower boundary and the line is the upper boundary. The region is shown below.\n\n![](images/e3/e32b8e7d6ce80d7cedad928f9a2f7acc3c3d7f06add7909e856db27d154068d6.png)\n\nThus\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le2,\\ x^2\\le y\\le x+2\\}.\n$$\n\nSince $x+2\\ge1$ on $D$, the logarithm and both component functions are smooth on an open neighborhood of this region. Applying Green's theorem yields\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r=\\int_{-1}^{2}\\int_{x^2}^{x+2}\\left(2x+3y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing $0$ as the outer lower limit would omit the part of the region to the left of the $y$-axis. Subtracting $2y$ instead of subtracting $-2y$ would give the wrong curl."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701215 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the boundary of the rectangle $D=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le2,\\ 0\\le y\\le3\\}$, traversed counterclockwise. Use Green's theorem in the form\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich repeated integral, integrating with respect to $y$ first, represents the following line integral? Do not evaluate it.\n\n$$\n\\oint_C y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}x+xy\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "For a counterclockwise, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve $C$ bounding $D$, Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe component functions here have continuous first partial derivatives on an open set containing $D$. Here $P=y^2$ and $Q=xy$, so\n\n$$\nP_y=2y,\\qquad Q_x=y,\\qquad Q_x-P_y=y-2y=-y.\n$$\n\nThe rectangle is shown below.\n\n![](images/6f/6f831b2b707e63e8eb34ff5f6c1c45d0267f51001de2560834d212506b48ee41.png)\n\nFor each fixed $x$, the inner variable $y$ runs from $0$ to $3$; the outer variable $x$ runs from $-1$ to $2$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\oint_C y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}x+xy\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\int_{-1}^{2}\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(-y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nAdding the two partial derivatives would give $3y$; Green's theorem requires their difference. The bounds must stay attached to their corresponding variables."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701217 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0)$, $(0,-1)$ and $(1,0)$, traversed counterclockwise. For\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle y^2+x^2,\\,xy+x^2\\rangle,\n$$\n\nuse Green's theorem to express $\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r$ as a repeated integral, integrating with respect to $x$ first. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For a counterclockwise, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve $C$ bounding $D$, Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe component functions here have continuous first partial derivatives on an open set containing $D$. The vector notation means\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r=(y^2+x^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+(xy+x^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nThus $P=y^2+x^2$ and $Q=xy+x^2$, giving\n\n$$\nP_y=2y,\\qquad Q_x=y+2x,\\qquad Q_x-P_y=2x-y.\n$$\n\nThe triangle and its counterclockwise boundary are shown below.\n\n![](images/6d/6d4f13abad5cc193dccedee3114fbd9cbd9fb8e6b5b3ddb0d07f0ed2ed51f4ff.png)\n\nThe sloping edge joins $(0,-1)$ to $(1,0)$, so its equation is $x=y+1$. At each height $-1\\le y\\le0$, a horizontal slice starts at $x=0$ and ends at $x=y+1$. Therefore\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le y\\le0,\\ 0\\le x\\le y+1\\},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r=\\int_{-1}^{0}\\int_{0}^{y+1}\\left(2x-y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nThe inner bounds depend on the outer variable $y$. Reversing the curl difference would reverse the sign even though the curve remains counterclockwise."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701230 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n\n$$\n\\int_C (- x - y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t, t - 2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe parameter interval is $0\\leq t\\leq 2$." :question/worked-solution "Use the scalar line-integral formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates and compute the speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle 1, 1\\rangle \\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(1)^2+(1)^2} \\\\\n&=\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\nSubstitute the coordinates into the density:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(\\mathbf r(t))&=(-1)\\left((t)+(t - 2)\\right) \\\\\n&=2 - 2 t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply by the speed and integrate, subtracting the lower endpoint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{2} (2 - 2 t)\\cdot (\\sqrt{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{2} - 2 \\sqrt{2} t + 2 \\sqrt{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[- \\sqrt{2} t^{2} + 2 \\sqrt{2} t\\right]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&=(0)-(0) \\\\\n&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701238 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n\n$$\n\\int_C (x y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3 t, 4 t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe parameter interval is $1\\leq t\\leq 2$." :question/worked-solution "Use the scalar line-integral formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates and compute the speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle 3, 4\\rangle \\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(3)^2+(4)^2} \\\\\n&=5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\nSubstitute the coordinates into the density:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(\\mathbf r(t))&=(1)(3 t)(4 t) \\\\\n&=12 t^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply by the speed and integrate, subtracting the lower endpoint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{2} (12 t^{2})\\cdot (5)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{1}^{2} 60 t^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[20 t^{3}\\right]_{1}^{2} \\\\\n&=(160)-(20) \\\\\n&=140\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701239 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n\n$$\n\\int_C (x y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 4 t, - 3 t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe parameter interval is $-1\\leq t\\leq 0$." :question/worked-solution "Use the scalar line-integral formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates and compute the speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle 4, -3\\rangle \\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(4)^2+(-3)^2} \\\\\n&=5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\nSubstitute the coordinates into the density:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(\\mathbf r(t))&=(1)(4 t)(- 3 t) \\\\\n&=- 12 t^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply by the speed and integrate, subtracting the lower endpoint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{-1}^{0} (- 12 t^{2})\\cdot (5)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{-1}^{0} - 60 t^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[- 20 t^{3}\\right]_{-1}^{0} \\\\\n&=(0)-(20) \\\\\n&=-20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701242 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(\\frac{2}{- x + y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle - t, - t - 2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe parameter interval is $1\\leq t\\leq 2$." :question/worked-solution "Use the scalar line-integral formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates and compute the speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle -1, -1\\rangle \\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(-1)^2+(-1)^2} \\\\\n&=\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\nSubstitute the coordinates into the density:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(\\mathbf r(t))&=\\frac{2}{(- t - 2)-(- t)} \\\\\n&=\\frac{2}{-2}=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply by the speed and integrate, subtracting the lower endpoint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{2} (-1)\\cdot (\\sqrt{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{1}^{2} - \\sqrt{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[- \\sqrt{2} t\\right]_{1}^{2} \\\\\n&=(- 2 \\sqrt{2})-(- \\sqrt{2}) \\\\\n&=- \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701259 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(\\frac{1}{- x + y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle - 2 t, - 2 t - 4\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe parameter interval is $1\\leq t\\leq 4$." :question/worked-solution "Use the scalar line-integral formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates and compute the speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle -2, -2\\rangle \\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(-2)^2+(-2)^2} \\\\\n&=2 \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\nSubstitute the coordinates into the density:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(\\mathbf r(t))&=\\frac{1}{(- 2 t - 4)-(- 2 t)} \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{-4}=- \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply by the speed and integrate, subtracting the lower endpoint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{4} \\left(- \\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\cdot (2 \\sqrt{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{1}^{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{\\sqrt{2} t}{2}\\right]_{1}^{4} \\\\\n&=(- 2 \\sqrt{2})-\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701284 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the boundary of the unit square, traversed counterclockwise, and let $D$ be its interior.\n\nUse Green's theorem to choose the function $f(x,y)$ in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\oint_C\\left(- x^{2} y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&\\quad+\\left(x^{3} y - 2 \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The stated traversal is **counterclockwise**. Green's theorem applies because both coefficients have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$. For this orientation,\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\n![The region and oriented boundary.](images/aa/aa60dd35a920d166f7cc3238a366bde93bd2c33e1b39b0a38313ab46a8de2d8b.png)\n\nIdentify the coefficients of the two differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y)&=- x^{2} y^{2},\\\\Q(x,y)&=x^{3} y - 2 \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHold the other variable constant when taking each partial derivative. Apply the power rule to each relevant term.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=- 2 x^{2} y,\\\\Q_x&=3 x^{2} y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the entire derivative in the order required by the traversal, then collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=Q_x-P_y\\\\&=\\left(3 x^{2} y\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(- 2 x^{2} y\\right)\\\\&=5 x^{2} y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $f(x,y)=5 x^{2} y$. No evaluation of the double integral is needed."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701286 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the boundary of the unit square, traversed counterclockwise, and let $D$ be its interior.\n\nUse Green's theorem to choose the function $f(x,y)$ in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\oint_C\\left(- y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&\\quad+\\left(x^{2} + x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The stated traversal is **counterclockwise**. Green's theorem applies because both coefficients have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$. For this orientation,\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\n![The region and oriented boundary.](images/aa/aa60dd35a920d166f7cc3238a366bde93bd2c33e1b39b0a38313ab46a8de2d8b.png)\n\nIdentify the coefficients of the two differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y)&=- y^{2},\\\\Q(x,y)&=x^{2} + x y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHold the other variable constant when taking each partial derivative. Apply the power rule to each relevant term.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=- 2 y,\\\\Q_x&=2 x + y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the entire derivative in the order required by the traversal, then collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=Q_x-P_y\\\\&=\\left(2 x + y\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(- 2 y\\right)\\\\&=2 x + 3 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $f(x,y)=2 x + 3 y$. No evaluation of the double integral is needed."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701296 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the boundary of the unit square, traversed counterclockwise, and let $D$ be its interior.\n\nUse Green's theorem to choose the function $f(x,y)$ in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\oint_C\\left(- y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&\\quad+\\left(- x^{2} + 3 x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The stated traversal is **counterclockwise**. Green's theorem applies because both coefficients have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$. For this orientation,\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\n![The region and oriented boundary.](images/aa/aa60dd35a920d166f7cc3238a366bde93bd2c33e1b39b0a38313ab46a8de2d8b.png)\n\nIdentify the coefficients of the two differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y)&=- y^{2},\\\\Q(x,y)&=- x^{2} + 3 x y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHold the other variable constant when taking each partial derivative. Apply the power rule to each relevant term.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=- 2 y,\\\\Q_x&=- 2 x + 3 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the entire derivative in the order required by the traversal, then collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=Q_x-P_y\\\\&=\\left(- 2 x + 3 y\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(- 2 y\\right)\\\\&=- 2 x + 5 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $f(x,y)=- 2 x + 5 y$. No evaluation of the double integral is needed."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701302 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the boundary of the unit square, traversed counterclockwise, and let $D$ be its interior.\n\nUse Green's theorem to choose the function $f(x,y)$ in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\oint_C\\left(- x^{2} y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&\\quad+\\left(- x^{3} y - \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The stated traversal is **counterclockwise**. Green's theorem applies because both coefficients have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$. For this orientation,\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\n![The region and oriented boundary.](images/aa/aa60dd35a920d166f7cc3238a366bde93bd2c33e1b39b0a38313ab46a8de2d8b.png)\n\nIdentify the coefficients of the two differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y)&=- x^{2} y^{2},\\\\Q(x,y)&=- x^{3} y - \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHold the other variable constant when taking each partial derivative. Apply the power rule to each relevant term.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=- 2 x^{2} y,\\\\Q_x&=- 3 x^{2} y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the entire derivative in the order required by the traversal, then collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=Q_x-P_y\\\\&=\\left(- 3 x^{2} y\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(- 2 x^{2} y\\right)\\\\&=- x^{2} y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $f(x,y)=- x^{2} y$. No evaluation of the double integral is needed."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701306 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(-1,0)$, $(1,0)$ and $(0,1)$, traversed counterclockwise, and let $D$ be its interior.\n\nUse Green's theorem to choose the function $f(x,y)$ in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\oint_C\\left(y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&\\quad+\\left(2 x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The stated traversal is **counterclockwise**. Green's theorem applies because both coefficients have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$. For this orientation,\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\n![The region and oriented boundary.](images/0d/0dfe132146c4095954d6b628b055317c975fed6ec429b9ad48b68f4015d6c033.png)\n\nIdentify the coefficients of the two differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y)&=y^{2},\\\\Q(x,y)&=2 x y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHold the other variable constant when taking each partial derivative. Apply the power rule to each relevant term.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=2 y,\\\\Q_x&=2 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the entire derivative in the order required by the traversal, then collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=Q_x-P_y\\\\&=\\left(2 y\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(2 y\\right)\\\\&=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $f(x,y)=0$. No evaluation of the double integral is needed."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701401 :question/problem "Let $D$ be the triangle with vertices $(-1,0)$, $(-1,3)$, $(0,3)$. Its boundary $C$ is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nWhich repeated integral, integrating with respect to $x$ first, represents the following line integral? Use the actual region $D$ with bounds in increasing order. Do not evaluate it.\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\left(- x y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- x^{2} y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The component functions are continuously differentiable on an open set containing $D$. For counterclockwise traversal, Green's theorem uses $Q_x-P_y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=- x y^{2},\\\\Q&=- x^{2} y,\\\\P_y&=- 2 x y,\\\\Q_x&=- 2 x y,\\\\Q_x-P_y&=(- 2 x y)-(- 2 x y)\\\\&=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sloping edge has equation $x=\\frac{y}{3} - 1$. The other edges give the constant limits.\n\nThus $D$ is described by $0\\le y\\le 3,\\quad -1\\le x\\le \\frac{y}{3} - 1$. The bounds stay attached to their variables.\n\n![](images/a0/a0e80193b025c92b7b40dc6f7d3b4dd462a81e5238da37de85881772f657cebb.png)\n\nThe required setup is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{-1}^{\\frac{y}{3} - 1}\\left(0\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701409 :question/problem "Let $D$ be the triangle with vertices $(0,0)$, $(3,0)$, $(3,1)$. Its boundary $C$ is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nWhich repeated integral, integrating with respect to $y$ first, represents the following line integral? Use the actual region $D$ with bounds in increasing order. Do not evaluate it.\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\left(- 2 x y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- x^{2} y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The component functions are continuously differentiable on an open set containing $D$. For counterclockwise traversal, Green's theorem uses $Q_x-P_y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=- 2 x y^{2},\\\\Q&=- x^{2} y,\\\\P_y&=- 4 x y,\\\\Q_x&=- 2 x y,\\\\Q_x-P_y&=(- 2 x y)-(- 4 x y)\\\\&=2 x y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sloping edge has equation $y=\\frac{x}{3}$. The other edges give the constant limits.\n\nThus $D$ is described by $0\\le x\\le 3,\\quad 0\\le y\\le \\frac{x}{3}$. The bounds stay attached to their variables.\n\n![](images/48/48de067ac9b9fac3432a6da3af0dd188ffa9fb603b9e97a418dca27d710e77bc.png)\n\nThe required setup is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{x}{3}}\\left(2 x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701416 :question/problem "Let $D$ be the triangle with vertices $(1,0)$, $(1,3)$, $(3,3)$. Its boundary $C$ is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nWhich repeated integral, integrating with respect to $x$ first, represents the following line integral? Use the actual region $D$ with bounds in increasing order. Do not evaluate it.\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\left(2 x y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- 2 x^{2} y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The component functions are continuously differentiable on an open set containing $D$. For counterclockwise traversal, Green's theorem uses $Q_x-P_y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=2 x y^{2},\\\\Q&=- 2 x^{2} y,\\\\P_y&=4 x y,\\\\Q_x&=- 4 x y,\\\\Q_x-P_y&=(- 4 x y)-(4 x y)\\\\&=- 8 x y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sloping edge has equation $x=\\frac{2 y}{3} + 1$. The other edges give the constant limits.\n\nThus $D$ is described by $0\\le y\\le 3,\\quad 1\\le x\\le \\frac{2 y}{3} + 1$. The bounds stay attached to their variables.\n\n![](images/09/095d4b0b71987085424b4e6cc587fb556563b5d14392fdc01b946ec47ef1a579.png)\n\nThe required setup is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{1}^{\\frac{2 y}{3} + 1}\\left(- 8 x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701418 :question/problem "Let $D$ be the triangle with vertices $(-1,-1)$, $(2,-1)$, $(2,1)$. Its boundary $C$ is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nWhich repeated integral, integrating with respect to $y$ first, represents the following line integral? Use the actual region $D$ with bounds in increasing order. Do not evaluate it.\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\left(- x y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(0\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The component functions are continuously differentiable on an open set containing $D$. For counterclockwise traversal, Green's theorem uses $Q_x-P_y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=- x y^{2},\\\\Q&=0,\\\\P_y&=- 2 x y,\\\\Q_x&=0,\\\\Q_x-P_y&=(0)-(- 2 x y)\\\\&=2 x y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sloping edge has equation $y=\\frac{2 x}{3} - \\frac{1}{3}$. The other edges give the constant limits.\n\nThus $D$ is described by $-1\\le x\\le 2,\\quad -1\\le y\\le \\frac{2 x}{3} - \\frac{1}{3}$. The bounds stay attached to their variables.\n\n![](images/21/21c1b915e4d05229cdb716819452717f681878820dab2bdcd6debed72c9045af.png)\n\nThe required setup is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=\\int_{-1}^{2}\\int_{-1}^{\\frac{2 x}{3} - \\frac{1}{3}}\\left(2 x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701423 :question/problem "Let $D$ be the triangle with vertices $(0,1)$, $(1,1)$, $(1,3)$. Its boundary $C$ is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nWhich repeated integral, integrating with respect to $y$ first, represents the following line integral? Use the actual region $D$ with bounds in increasing order. Do not evaluate it.\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\left(x y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(2 x^{2} y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The component functions are continuously differentiable on an open set containing $D$. For counterclockwise traversal, Green's theorem uses $Q_x-P_y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=x y^{2},\\\\Q&=2 x^{2} y,\\\\P_y&=2 x y,\\\\Q_x&=4 x y,\\\\Q_x-P_y&=(4 x y)-(2 x y)\\\\&=2 x y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sloping edge has equation $y=2 x + 1$. The other edges give the constant limits.\n\nThus $D$ is described by $0\\le x\\le 1,\\quad 1\\le y\\le 2 x + 1$. The bounds stay attached to their variables.\n\n![](images/6d/6d2357fd4e88b6ab19b461986600c264510b88d0524578133658291d1eb7005c.png)\n\nThe required setup is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{1}^{2 x + 1}\\left(2 x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701626 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=2 t - 2,\\quad y=(t - 4) (t + 3),\\quad t\\in\\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation is\n\n$y=$ {{field-1}}$,\\quad x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$" :question/worked-solution "Eliminate the parameter by using the $x$ equation to isolate $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x &=2 t - 2 \\\\ t &=\\frac{x}{2} + 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $t=\\frac{x}{2} + 1$ into the original $y$ expression, then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=((\\frac{x}{2} + 1) - 4) ((\\frac{x}{2} + 1) + 3) \\\\ &=(\\frac{x}{2} - 3) (\\frac{x}{2} + 4) \\\\ &=\\frac{(x - 6) (x + 8)}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe affine $x$ equation attains every real value, so $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n\nTherefore the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x - 6) (x + 8)}{4},\\quad x\\in\\mathbb{R}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701627 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{2} - \\frac{t}{2},\\quad y=- (t - 4) (t^{2} - 25),\\quad t\\in\\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation is\n\n$y=$ {{field-1}}$,\\quad x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$" :question/worked-solution "Eliminate the parameter by using the $x$ equation to isolate $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{5}{2} - \\frac{t}{2} \\\\ t &=5 - 2 x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $t=5 - 2 x$ into the original $y$ expression, then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=-(((5 - 2 x) - 4) ((5 - 2 x)^{2} - 25)) \\\\ &=- (1 - 2 x) ((5 - 2 x)^{2} - 25) \\\\ &=2 x (1 - 2 x) (10 - 2 x) \\\\ &=4 x (x - 5) (2 x - 1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe affine $x$ equation attains every real value, so $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n\nTherefore the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=4 x (x - 5) (2 x - 1),\\quad x\\in\\mathbb{R}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701631 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=- 2 t - 5,\\quad y=- (t - 2) (t + 1),\\quad t\\in\\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation is\n\n$y=$ {{field-1}}$,\\quad x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$" :question/worked-solution "Eliminate the parameter by using the $x$ equation to isolate $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x &=- 2 t - 5 \\\\ t &=- \\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $t=- \\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{5}{2}$ into the original $y$ expression, then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=-(((- \\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{5}{2}) - 2) ((- \\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{5}{2}) + 1)) \\\\ &=- (- \\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{9}{2}) (- \\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{3}{2}) \\\\ &=- \\frac{(x + 3) (x + 9)}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe affine $x$ equation attains every real value, so $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n\nTherefore the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=- \\frac{(x + 3) (x + 9)}{4},\\quad x\\in\\mathbb{R}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701636 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=- 3 t - 1,\\quad y=t (t + 3),\\quad t\\in\\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation is\n\n$y=$ {{field-1}}$,\\quad x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$" :question/worked-solution "Eliminate the parameter by using the $x$ equation to isolate $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x &=- 3 t - 1 \\\\ t &=- \\frac{x}{3} - \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $t=- \\frac{x}{3} - \\frac{1}{3}$ into the original $y$ expression, then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=(- \\frac{x}{3} - \\frac{1}{3}) ((- \\frac{x}{3} - \\frac{1}{3}) + 3) \\\\ &=(\\frac{8}{3} - \\frac{x}{3}) (- \\frac{x}{3} - \\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ &=\\frac{(x - 8) (x + 1)}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe affine $x$ equation attains every real value, so $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n\nTherefore the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x - 8) (x + 1)}{9},\\quad x\\in\\mathbb{R}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701638 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t}{2} - \\frac{5}{2},\\quad y=- (t + 1)^{2} (t^{2} - 1),\\quad t\\in\\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation is\n\n$y=$ {{field-1}}$,\\quad x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$" :question/worked-solution "Eliminate the parameter by using the $x$ equation to isolate $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{t}{2} - \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ t &=2 x + 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $t=2 x + 5$ into the original $y$ expression, then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=-(((2 x + 5) + 1)^{2} ((2 x + 5)^{2} - 1)) \\\\ &=- (2 x + 6)^{2} ((2 x + 5)^{2} - 1) \\\\ &=- (2 x + 4) (2 x + 6)^{3} \\\\ &=- 16 (x + 2) (x + 3)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe affine $x$ equation attains every real value, so $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n\nTherefore the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=- 16 (x + 2) (x + 3)^{3},\\quad x\\in\\mathbb{R}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701981 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\sin t,\\qquad y=\\cos t-4,\\qquad t\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n{{field-1}}$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx=2\\sin t & \\Rightarrow\\quad \\sin t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\\ny=\\cos t-4 & \\Rightarrow\\quad \\cos t=y + 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^2+\\left(y + 4\\right)^2 & =1 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{4}x^2+\\left(y + 4\\right)^2 & =1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe traced curve also satisfies $-2\\le x\\le 2,\\quad -5\\le y\\le -3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701984 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=6\\cot t,\\qquad y=\\csc t,\\qquad 0<t<\\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx=6\\cot t & \\Rightarrow\\quad \\cot t=\\frac{x}{6} \\\\\ny=\\csc t & \\Rightarrow\\quad \\csc t=y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t=1$ by $\\sin^2 t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\sin^2 t}{\\sin^2 t}+\\frac{\\cos^2 t}{\\sin^2 t} & =\\frac{1}{\\sin^2 t} \\\\\n1+\\left(\\frac{\\cos t}{\\sin t}\\right)^2 & =\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sin t}\\right)^2 \\\\\n1+\\cot^2 t & =\\csc^2 t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cot t=\\frac{x}{6}$ and $\\csc t=y$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n1+\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)^2=y^2.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y^2=1+\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)^2.$\n\nExpanding the products or squaring the coordinate fractions gives\n\n$$\ny^{2}=\\frac{x^{2}}{36} + 1.\n$$\n\nThe traced curve also satisfies $x>0,\\quad y>1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701988 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=4\\cot t,\\qquad y=5\\csc t,\\qquad 0<t<\\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx=4\\cot t & \\Rightarrow\\quad \\cot t=\\frac{x}{4} \\\\\ny=5\\csc t & \\Rightarrow\\quad \\csc t=\\frac{y}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity $\\csc^2 t=1+\\cot^2 t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\csc^2 t & =1+\\cot^2 t \\\\\n\\frac{y^2}{25} & =1+\\frac{x^2}{16}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used here that $\\csc t>0$ and $\\cot t>0$ in the range $0<t<\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\nNow, solving for $y$, we obtain\n\n$$\ny=5\\sqrt{1+\\frac{x^2}{16}}.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=5\\sqrt{1+\\frac{x^2}{16}}.$\n\nThe traced curve also satisfies $x>0,\\quad y>5$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701990 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=6\\tan t,\\qquad y=\\sec t,\\qquad 0<t<\\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx=6\\tan t & \\Rightarrow\\quad \\tan t=\\frac{x}{6} \\\\\ny=\\sec t & \\Rightarrow\\quad \\sec t=y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t=1$ by $\\cos^2 t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\sin^2 t}{\\cos^2 t}+\\frac{\\cos^2 t}{\\cos^2 t} & =\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 t} \\\\\n\\left(\\frac{\\sin t}{\\cos t}\\right)^2+1 & =\\left(\\frac{1}{\\cos t}\\right)^2 \\\\\n\\tan^2 t+1 & =\\sec^2 t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\tan t=\\frac{x}{6}$ and $\\sec t=y$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)^2+1=y^2.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y^2=\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)^2+1.$\n\nExpanding the products or squaring the coordinate fractions gives\n\n$$\ny^{2}=\\frac{x^{2}}{36} + 1.\n$$\n\nThe traced curve also satisfies $x>0,\\quad y>1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701992 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos t-3,\\qquad y=3\\sin t-2,\\qquad t\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx=3\\cos t-3 & \\Rightarrow\\quad \\cos t=\\frac{x + 3}{3} \\\\\ny=3\\sin t-2 & \\Rightarrow\\quad \\sin t=\\frac{y + 2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{y + 2}{3}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{x + 3}{3}\\right)^2=1.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $\\left(\\frac{y + 2}{3}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{x + 3}{3}\\right)^2=1.$\n\nExpanding the products or squaring the coordinate fractions gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\left(x + 3\\right)^{2}}{9} + \\frac{\\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}}{9}=1.\n$$\n\nThe traced curve also satisfies $-6\\le x\\le 0,\\quad -5\\le y\\le 1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186702015 :question/problem "If\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthen $\\det(B)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Expand across the top row, using the signs $+,-,+$. Each minor is the $2\\times2$ determinant left after deleting the entry's row and column. Write $M_{1j}$ for the minor of the entry in column $j$ of the top row.\n\nThe third top-row entry is zero, so its term vanishes. The other two minors are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M_{11}&=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}=1\\cdot3-2\\cdot(-2)=7, \\\\ M_{12}&=\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}=(-3)\\cdot3-2\\cdot1=-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the top-row expansion:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det(B)&=2M_{11}-(-1)M_{12}+0M_{13} \\\\ &=2(7)-(-1)(-11) \\\\ &=14-11 \\\\ &=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the negative middle entry gives a positive multiplier, but its minor is $-11$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186702017 :question/problem "If\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthen $\\det(C)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Expand across the top row, using the signs $+,-,+$. Each minor is the $2\\times2$ determinant left after deleting the entry's row and column. Write $M_{1j}$ for the minor of the entry in column $j$ of the top row.\n\nAll three terms must be evaluated. First calculate the minors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M_{11}&=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=2\\cdot1-(-2)(-3)=-4, \\\\ M_{12}&=\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=(-1)\\cdot1-(-2)\\cdot3=5, \\\\ M_{13}&=\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}=(-1)(-3)-2\\cdot3=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen combine the signed contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det(C)&=2M_{11}-(-1)M_{12}+3M_{13} \\\\ &=2(-4)-(-1)(5)+3(-3) \\\\ &=-8+5-9 \\\\ &=-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKeep the sign of each entry separate from the alternating expansion signs. In particular, the middle contribution is $+5$, while the last contribution is $-9$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186702022 :question/problem "Find $\\det(A)$ for\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Expand across the top row, using the signs $+,-,+$. Each minor is the $2\\times2$ determinant left after deleting the entry's row and column.\n\nThe first and third terms vanish because their top-row entries are zero. Thus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det(A)&=-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=2\\bigl(1\\cdot5-4\\cdot2\\bigr) \\\\ &=2(5-8) \\\\ &=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe negative middle entry and the minus sign in the expansion give a positive multiplier; the minor itself is negative."}]}
